WEBVTT

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.560
I'm your host, Audrey Staples.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:24.820
You might have heard the saying, the opposites attract.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:27.640
And you know, that's true in a lot of cases,

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:29.840
like in some bad romantic comedy.

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:33.250
or when you bring a pair of magnets close to each other.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.200
North and south poles will snap together.

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:38.750
But sometimes, like with water,

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:41.370
molecules of the same substance attract.

00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:42.530
Wonder why?

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:44.440
You'll get a better picture of why

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:46.220
when you get the chance to see water

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:47.780
someplace other than Earth,

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.440
like on the International Space Station,

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:52.460
commonly referred to as the ISS,

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:55.170
where water appears to be nearly weightless.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:58.880
Here's Canadian Space Agency astronaut Bob Thurks

00:00:58.900 --> 00:00:59.680
on the ISS.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.980
with a closer look at water and this special property.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:08.240
The intermolecular forces cause water to be attracted to itself, which is called cohesion.

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:11.180
For example, here are two spheres of water.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:17.240
When the spheres are several centimeters apart, there's no attractive forces between them.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:27.640
But, when I bring them close enough together to attract, they merge through their attractive forces.

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:29.970
So, you probably already...

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.460
that water molecules are attracted to other water molecules and that's called

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:41.060
cohesion. Well these same cohesive forces are responsible for surface tension.

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:46.430
Surface tension is a property of a liquid that makes it beat up when it drips on

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:51.580
the surface like a leaf or your skin or a coin. Water surface tension is pretty

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:56.680
strong. You can check it out with an eyedropper of water and a penny. Take a

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:57.360
Take a closer look.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:01:59.960
See what happens when you squeeze the water.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.420
out onto the penny, one drop at a time. How many drops do you think you can

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:10.300
gently squeeze onto the penny before the water spills over the side of the penny?

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:15.520
I bet it'll be more than you expected. Surface tension is what allows the water

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:21.240
drops to cling together, forming that dome or bulge at the top. You can see that

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:25.960
water surface tension is pretty strong. You can also see the cohesion of the

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:29.860
water as it stuck to itself but the water did something else

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.380
well. It's stuck to the penny, too. Why was that? Here's Bob again to explain and to show

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:40.480
how it works in space. Intermolecular forces can also cause water to be attracted to other

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:54.920
materials. This is called adhesion. For example, water can stick to a straw. When I move the

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:58.940
straw close to the sphere, the water sticks.

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:07.900
Water sticks to many other materials as well.

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:11.500
Think about all the things on earth that you see little water drops on.

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:14.720
Windows, tree leaves, even your own skin.

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:21.960
Let me show you with my hand, the molecules in my skin will pull hard enough to make the water stick.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:29.280
The water tries to find a balance between the cohesion with the water and the adhesion to my hand.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:36.960
eventually it settles on an unusual shape that fills the space between my fingers.

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:45.780
If I continue by adding water, I can eventually build an entire glove of water

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:57.570
that will stay fixed to my hand. It sticks to my hand by intermolecular forces. How cool is that?

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:06.870
for gravity, subtle forces like surface tension, cohesion, and adhesion might have a lot bigger

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:09.230
impact on our everyday lives on Earth.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:12.350
Would we still use bowls, spoons, and cups?

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:14.980
Or would we come up with new tools that would...

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:20.070
make our lives easier. Well, that's something to think about as we plan to explore places

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:25.180
like the moon, Mars and beyond. That's it for now, I'm Audrey Staples and we'll catch

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:26.900
you next time on NASA Launch Pad.
